<font size=5>1997 Monitoring Report</font>
<br>
<i>Steve BauerPocket Water, Inc. (PWI)</i>
<p>
A major effort in August was completing the 1997 Monitoring Report.  The report is divided into two sections, the main body and the appendices, to facilitate printing and transfer over the Internet.  
<p>
Using data from IDFG 1997 snorkel surveys and redd counts, we added a brief section on fish populations.
<p>
The section on channel morphology compares changes in channel length, gradient, sinuosity, and residual pool depth in the Phase I and II construction areas.  Gloria (UI) produced a series of maps in ArcView and calculated the residual pools depths for this report.
<p>
Data on plant vegetation survival over the winter of 1997-98, collected by Kurt (WHI), are included and two photopoint series are shown in the appendix.
<p>
Lessons learned from evaluating data for the report and observations from field surveys will be used to update the overall monitoring plan.  We would like to hear from the TAC members regarding any suggested changes to the monitoring methods or evaluation procedures.


	</td>
	<td width="50%" valign="top">
	
<font size=5>Background Data/Literature Search</font>
<br>
<i>UI Eco-Hydraulics Group (UI)</i>
<p>

Historic photos of the Red River meadow for the years 1955, '65, '79, '92, and '96 were ordered from the USDA FSA Aerial Photography Field Office in Salt Lake City.  The black and white photos of the earlier years are 24 in. x 24 in. and should be available in late September.
<p>
Zane Fulbright (archeologist with the Red River Ranger District) helped Steve Clayton locate entry survey numbers for homesteads on the Red River meadow.  Steve will follow up with the BLM State office in Boise and Smithsonian Museum in Washington, D.C. to obtain the original homestead "inspection reports," dating back to 1908.
<p>
Initial searches for background data at the Idaho Historical Museum Archives have not uncovered any telling photographs; however, oral histories from the Elk City area (taped in 1981) are being transcribed and may reveal more information.
<p>
A literature survey continues for background information that could be used in the design of Phase III of the current restoration project, as well as any future extensions of the project beyond the IDFG property.

